import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.C3idr2hZ.js";const d="/orc/screens/settings-providers-desktop.png",n="/orc/screens/settings-usage.png",k=JSON.parse('{"title":"Configuration","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"docs/configuration.md","filePath":"docs/configuration.md","lastUpdated":1794606014000}'),i={name:"docs/configuration.md"};function r(c,e,l,h,p,u){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="configuration" tabindex="-1">Configuration <a class="header-anchor" href="#configuration" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Configuration&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="coding-providers" tabindex="-1">Coding providers <a class="header-anchor" href="#coding-providers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Coding providers&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Open <strong>Settings → Providers</strong>. Choose <strong>New provider</strong>, name it (for example, <strong>Claude LiteLLM</strong>), select a coding-agent adapter, and configure its connection. You can create several providers using the same adapter. Claude Code and Codex use native integrations; Pi uses its native RPC interface, OpenCode uses direct ACP, and other coding agents use acpx. Field guidance and examples live behind info icons: hover, focus or tap to read them.</p><p>Choose an enabled <strong>Default provider</strong> for new chats. Each provider has a default model; leave it blank to use the adapter&#39;s default. The chat picker selects named providers and can override the model and launch options for that conversation. An explicit choice in this browser is remembered across reloads; saving provider settings clears that browser override so the saved defaults take effect. Unsent model and option edits are retained separately for each provider while switching. Switching to another provider of the <strong>same</strong> agent keeps the conversation: ORC rebuilds the native session for the new account on the next turn and replays a bounded transcript of the chat so you can continue where you left off. Switching to a different agent starts a new conversation. Existing conversations retain their provider ID and model when the default changes. Disabling a provider hides it from new launches while allowing its existing conversations to resume. Conversations saved before profiles existed remain pinned to their original native provider.</p><p><img src="'+d+`" alt="Named coding providers with demo data"></p><p><a href="/orc/screens/settings-providers-phone.png">Phone layout</a> · <a href="/orc/screens/provider-compaction-phone.png">Compact context action</a></p><p>For Claude, Codex, OpenCode and Pi, <strong>Executable path</strong> overrides the local binary for native chat, model discovery and provider terminal launches. Enter a path without arguments, or leave it blank for the default installation. Paths with spaces and <code>~/</code> work. SSH uses the remote installation. Changes apply at the next launch; ORC does not restart running processes.</p><p><strong>Advanced configuration</strong> starts collapsed:</p><ul><li><strong>Environment overrides</strong> apply to launches and native model discovery, including SSH. Values override the execution environment; an empty value clears an inherited value. Use variables such as <code>CODEX_HOME</code> or <code>CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR</code> for a separate CLI configuration, or set <strong>Config directory</strong> as described below. An explicit environment row wins over that directory. ORC does not create login credentials or copy an existing login into a new directory.</li><li>Mark sensitive values <strong>Secret</strong>. Credential names such as <code>ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN</code> require this automatically. Secrets are encrypted on the API host and are write-only in the UI. An untouched saved placeholder preserves its value; entering a replacement changes it. Remove a row to stop overriding that variable. Transport and ORC connection variables are managed by ORC.</li><li><strong>Chat launch options (JSON)</strong> accept validated adapter options: Claude SDK options such as <code>{&quot;effort&quot;:&quot;high&quot;,&quot;maxTurns&quot;:20}</code>, or supported Codex/acpx argument arrays. ORC owns session, transport and working-folder arguments. Explicit session options take precedence over provider defaults. Native terminal launches use provider environment and, for Claude/Codex, model; chat JSON options are for the chat adapter. Package setups keep their own launch configuration.</li></ul><h3 id="separate-claude-and-codex-accounts" tabindex="-1">Separate Claude and Codex accounts <a class="header-anchor" href="#separate-claude-and-codex-accounts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Separate Claude and Codex accounts&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Create another provider with the same adapter to work with more than one Claude Code or Codex login. While creating the provider, enable <strong>Use a separate account</strong>, or open an existing provider and set <strong>Config directory</strong>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Adapter</th><th>Variable</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Claude Code</td><td><code>CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR</code></td><td><code>~/.claude</code></td></tr><tr><td>Codex</td><td><code>CODEX_HOME</code></td><td><code>~/.codex</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Each directory holds that account&#39;s CLI login, session history and state. The provider passes it to native chat, model discovery and terminal launches for Claude/Codex profiles; other adapters ignore the field. <code>~/</code> expands on the API host, so use an absolute path for an SSH execution host. Leave it blank to use the host default. An explicit <code>CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR</code> or <code>CODEX_HOME</code> environment row still wins.</p><p>The provider card reads the account label, plan and organization from the CLI&#39;s own files on the API host and shows a copyable sign-in command. Run that command once in a terminal, sign in, then select the provider in the chat picker. ORC never stores, refreshes, relays or returns credentials. Two providers that point at the same login are flagged so a switch does not silently reuse one account. <strong>Use for new sessions</strong> saves that provider as the default without opening the full form.</p><p><strong>Settings → Usage</strong> shows a quota card per configured Claude/Codex provider and for Alibaba Cloud when the Bailian CLI (<code>bl</code>) is installed, and session discovery, the live-session watcher and transcript search include every configured directory. Remote SSH accounts are not inspected: identity, quotas and session discovery for separate accounts currently read the local API host only. ORC does not rotate accounts automatically when a quota is exhausted; switch the provider in the picker and continue.</p><p>Use <strong>Save changes</strong> to save the provider list, default and advanced settings together. Drafts survive switching providers and Settings sections. <strong>Use default</strong> clears an executable override; <strong>Discard changes</strong> restores saved values. Failed saves retain the draft. Concurrent edits are rejected rather than overwriting another device&#39;s settings; <strong>Discard draft and reload</strong> loads the saved version.</p><h3 id="pi" tabindex="-1">Pi <a class="header-anchor" href="#pi" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pi&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Install <a href="https://pi.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Pi</a> 1.5.2 or newer on the execution host, with Node.js 22.19 or newer, and configure its model credentials in Pi. Create a provider in <strong>Settings → Providers</strong> and choose <strong>Pi</strong>. Pi is optional and is not enabled by default. ORC uses <code>pi --mode rpc</code>, preserving Pi&#39;s own credentials, extensions, skills and session files. An executable override selects the local Pi installation; SSH uses the remote installation.</p><p>The model picker discovers configured models without an inference turn. Use <code>provider/model</code> to distinguish identical model IDs from different providers. <strong>Chat launch options (JSON)</strong> accepts an object such as:</p><div class="language-json vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">json</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">{</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">  &quot;thinkingLevel&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;high&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">  &quot;steeringMode&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;one-at-a-time&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">  &quot;followUpMode&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;one-at-a-time&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">  &quot;autoCompaction&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">true</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">  &quot;autoRetry&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">true</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">}</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Thinking levels are <code>off</code>, <code>minimal</code>, <code>low</code>, <code>medium</code>, <code>high</code>, <code>xhigh</code> and <code>max</code>; Pi applies the selected model&#39;s limits. Queue modes are <code>all</code> or <code>one-at-a-time</code>. These controls also work as native Pi options for agent jobs using backend <code>pi</code>. Queue mode, auto-retry and auto-compaction changes are saved to Pi&#39;s own settings on the execution host, so external Pi sessions use them too. Thinking level is scoped to the ORC session. ORC owns transport, session identity, working folder and its permission bridge; arbitrary Pi command-line arguments are not accepted.</p><p>Pi supports streamed text/thinking and tool results, images on compatible models, native session resume, <strong>Send now</strong> steering, cooperative Stop, extension questions, permission prompts, live permission changes and <code>/compact</code>. Default permissions ask before each tool; <strong>Accept edits</strong> allows Pi&#39;s edit/write tools, and bypass permits all tools. An approval for the session applies to that tool name. This gates Pi&#39;s tool calls; it is not an operating-system sandbox or a restriction on extension code. Plan permissions and ORC tool whitelists are rejected rather than silently ignored.</p><p>With ORC tools enabled, ORC registers an authenticated, scoped MCP connection through a temporary session extension. It waits for tools to connect and refuses to start if the bridge fails. It does not edit <code>~/.pi/agent/mcp.json</code> or <code>.pi/mcp.json</code>. Resume uses Pi&#39;s exact native session UUID; missing sessions produce an error instead of creating another conversation. Keep the execution host and its Pi session directory available for saved conversations.</p><p>Pi&#39;s terminal-only custom components and editor replacement are not reproduced in ORC. Native Pi sessions are discovered and can resume in chat or a terminal; see <a href="./live-sessions">Live agent sessions</a> for session directories and provider selection. Fork/rollback and native account quota controls are not exposed. Active ORC rules that require unavailable interception prevent the session from starting.</p><h3 id="bedrock-and-litellm-examples" tabindex="-1">Bedrock and LiteLLM examples <a class="header-anchor" href="#bedrock-and-litellm-examples" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Bedrock and LiteLLM examples&quot;">​</a></h3><p>These are environment configurations of the Claude adapter:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Configuration</th><th>Environment overrides</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Claude Bedrock</td><td><code>CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK=1</code>, <code>AWS_REGION</code>, and an <code>AWS_PROFILE</code> configured on the execution host</td></tr><tr><td>Claude LiteLLM</td><td><code>ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL</code> pointing to your gateway and a secret <code>ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Choose the model ID supported by your gateway or Bedrock deployment. If an inherited <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code> conflicts with gateway authentication, add an empty secret override for it. AWS profiles, gateway routing and optional endpoint variables remain native Claude configuration; see <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/amazon-bedrock" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Claude on Bedrock</a>, <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/llm-gateway" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Claude gateway configuration</a> and <a href="https://docs.litellm.ai/docs/proxy/client_setup/claude_code" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">LiteLLM&#39;s Claude Code guide</a>. The configured URL is reached from the execution host, so a loopback proxy must run on that host.</p><h3 id="storage-and-api" tabindex="-1">Storage and API <a class="header-anchor" href="#storage-and-api" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Storage and API&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Authenticated <code>GET/PUT /api/settings/providers</code> reads and replaces <code>{providers, default_provider_id, revision}</code>. Each provider has <code>id</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>agent</code>, <code>enabled</code>, <code>executable</code>, optional <code>home</code>, <code>model</code>, optional <code>options</code>, and <code>environment: [{name, value, secret}]</code>. <code>home</code> is the CLI config directory used by Claude Code and Codex providers; blank or absent means the host default. Secret values are returned as <code>null</code>; send that placeholder unchanged to retain a saved secret with the same provider and variable name. New or renamed secrets require a value. Send the current revision when saving; stale writes return 409. An existing provider&#39;s adapter is immutable; create another provider to change adapters.</p><p>Authenticated <code>GET /api/settings/providers/accounts</code> returns the account identity ORC can read from each provider&#39;s config directory: status, label, organization, plan, duplicate marker and a sign-in command. It never returns tokens or credential contents.</p><p>Private configuration lives under <code>providers.instances</code> and <code>providers.default_id</code> in the server&#39;s JSON config. Encrypted values use ORC&#39;s machine key at <code>ORC_HOME/secret.key</code>; back up that key with the config. Provider secrets are excluded from readable workspace preferences. Existing <code>preferences.agents</code>, <code>preferences.provider_paths</code> and chat defaults are migrated when saving Providers and remain compatible with <code>GET/PATCH /api/settings</code>. Without saved profiles, the existing CLI login, paths and chat settings are used.</p><p><strong>Local account limits</strong> starts collapsed. <strong>Check limits</strong> reuses the local-account quota reader below; it does not report gateway/Bedrock billing or SSH quotas. There is no provider health polling. Native Claude quota rejections and Codex usage-window notices include reset times when supplied by the agent. Claude extra usage is distinguished from a blocked request. A failed turn holds queued messages; retry explicitly after access returns. ORC does not switch accounts automatically or schedule quota retries; use the chat picker to move the open conversation to another account of the same agent.</p><h2 id="project-workspace-environment" tabindex="-1">Project workspace environment <a class="header-anchor" href="#project-workspace-environment" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Project workspace environment&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Open a project and use <strong>Edit → Workspace</strong>, or fill the <strong>Workspace</strong> step while creating a project. These settings apply to terminals, flow-node sessions, chat turns and job actions that run in that project&#39;s folders.</p><ul><li><strong>Setup script</strong> runs once in each new managed worktree under <code>.orc/worktrees</code>, <code>.claude/worktrees</code> or <code>.codex/worktrees</code>, before the first agent or terminal starts there. ORC runs it with the project&#39;s shell on the terminal&#39;s host, from the worktree folder. A failed or timed-out script is recorded with its output and the agent still starts. The script does not rerun for an existing worktree, and a project&#39;s main checkout is never prepared for you. <strong>Setup timeout</strong> is 900 seconds by default (30–3600).</li><li><strong>Dev script</strong> starts with <strong>Start dev server</strong> in the terminal launcher. It opens as a shell terminal in the current folder; the first printed <code>http(s)</code> URL becomes the workspace preview link. Loopback hosts such as <code>0.0.0.0</code> and <code>::</code> are shown as <code>localhost</code>, and later URLs in the same terminal are ignored.</li><li><strong>Port range</strong> gives each workspace a stable block instead of a single port. The first workspace of a project starts at 3100 with 10 ports unless <strong>Port range start</strong> or <strong>Ports per workspace</strong> is changed (1-100). Later workspaces take the next free block, and blocks never overlap on the same API host, even across projects. Ports already bound on a local host are skipped; SSH hosts cannot be probed, so only ORC&#39;s own allocations are avoided there.</li><li><strong>Environment</strong> holds extra variables for every launch in the project. Mark sensitive values <strong>Secret</strong> to encrypt them with ORC&#39;s machine key; secrets are write-only and read back as empty placeholders. An untouched placeholder keeps the saved value. <code>ORC_</code> names are reserved for ORC.</li></ul><p>Workspaces export <code>PORT</code>, <code>ORC_PORT</code>, <code>ORC_PORT_END</code>, <code>ORC_PORT_COUNT</code> and <code>ORC_PORT_1</code>…<code>ORC_PORT_&lt;count&gt;</code>, plus <code>ORC_PROJECT_ID</code> and <code>ORC_WORKSPACE</code> (<code>ORC_WORKTREE</code> only inside a managed worktree). Project environment values are added last.</p><p>Setup status, ports and the preview link appear in a session&#39;s inspector and on a task&#39;s Git links, and are readable through <code>GET /api/workspaces</code>. The preview belongs to the workspace path and is cleared when its dev terminal exits. Deleting a project removes its workspace rows. Flow nodes run in the task&#39;s checkout; per-node worktrees are not created yet.</p><h2 id="manual-context-compaction" tabindex="-1">Manual context compaction <a class="header-anchor" href="#manual-context-compaction" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Manual context compaction&quot;">​</a></h2><p>For an existing idle Claude, Codex or Pi chat, choose <strong>Compact context</strong> from session actions (an icon in the expanded desktop header), or send <code>/compact</code>. This uses the same native session: Claude&#39;s SDK command, Codex&#39;s <code>thread/compact/start</code> RPC, or Pi&#39;s <code>compact</code> RPC. Progress, failures and completion use the normal conversation stream and history. Running turns, attachments and integrations without native support cannot be compacted this way. Keep automatic compaction under the agent&#39;s own control; Claude sessions with slash commands explicitly disabled must enable them first. There is no ORC idle-compaction timer. Compaction summarizes model context and may use provider quota; it does not erase the displayed ORC transcript.</p><h2 id="usage-in-settings" tabindex="-1">Usage in Settings <a class="header-anchor" href="#usage-in-settings" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Usage in Settings&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Open <strong>Settings → Usage</strong> for request-level token usage and account quotas. ORC indexes each assistant request recorded by the local Claude, Codex, Pi, OpenCode, Gemini and Cursor importers: model, request time, input, output, cache-read, cache-write, reasoning and unsplit totals. The page groups that ledger by agent, model and UTC day or hour, so the selected period reflects tokens consumed during the period rather than lifetime totals of sessions that started in it.</p><p>Filters narrow the page by period (24 hours, 7, 30 or 90 days, or all time), time bucket, agent and model. The distribution table shows model shares and expands a model to its token split and request, session and estimate counts. Clicking an agent filters the page to that agent. The default view keeps long lists compact; secondary detail opens on demand and the account quota list starts collapsed.</p><p>Gemini records only a per-message total, and Cursor exposes a context estimate rather than per-request usage, so those requests are marked estimated. Codex rollouts larger than the import cap keep their lifetime total as an estimated remainder, and model names are unknown for that remainder. Claude output splits thinking tokens out of output, and cached input is counted once. Repeated assistant messages are counted once by message id. The Coverage section reports sessions started in the period that have not been indexed yet and excludes their lifetime totals from the distribution, rather than mixing the two accounting models. No billed cost is shown: ORC has no reliable price source for every provider and plan.</p><p>Raw requests are kept for 95 days, just past the 90-day window, so every normal period stays exact. Older usage rolls up per session, UTC day and model: totals, model/agent shares, request counts and session counts survive for <strong>All time</strong> without keeping every request. A single session that exceeds 5,000 rows inside the window also compacts its oldest rows by model. Retention runs on the normal session-sync path, not on a timer or a page poll.</p><p><img src="`+n+'" alt="Usage in Settings with demo data"></p><p>Account quotas show percentage used and reset times for local Codex and Claude accounts on the API host, one card per configured provider. ORC reads the existing CLI login from each provider&#39;s config directory—<code>CODEX_HOME/auth.json</code> (default <code>~/.codex/auth.json</code>) or <code>CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/.credentials.json</code> (default <code>~/.claude/.credentials.json</code>)—and requests quota data from the provider over HTTPS. Credentials stay on the server and are never returned to the browser. Keychain-only, API-key and SSH-host logins are not supported. Missing logins, expired access and unsupported provider responses display an unavailable state. Sign in again using the provider CLI when access expires; ORC does not refresh or modify provider credentials.</p><p>When the Bailian CLI (<code>bl</code>) is on the API host&#39;s <code>PATH</code>, Usage also shows an Alibaba Cloud card. ORC runs <code>bl usage token-plan --output json</code> with an 8-second timeout and displays the reported monthly token-plan percentage and reset time. Only the percentage and reset timestamp are returned to the browser; the CLI&#39;s stored credentials are not read by ORC. An account without an active token plan, a missing login and an unreachable CLI all display an unavailable state. Alibaba Cloud coding-plan quotas are not shown.</p><p>The authenticated read-only routes <code>GET /api/usage/report</code>, <code>GET /api/usage/history</code> and <code>GET /api/usage/quotas</code> expose the same data. Report accepts <code>days=1|7|30|90|0</code>, <code>granularity=day|hour</code>, and optional <code>agent</code> and <code>model</code> filters; model <code>__unknown__</code> selects requests without a model. History remains available for clients that need lifetime totals by session start date. Quota requests share a 60-second server cache and an 8-second provider timeout. The Usage panel refreshes itself from the shared SSE change stream when newly indexed requests arrive; it does not poll. Refresh re-reads both views on demand and respects the quota cache. Provider quota interfaces can change; unavailable quota data does not prevent usage from loading.</p><p>Where ORC keeps its config and data, every config key and environment variable you are likely to set, logs, running as a background service, and authentication.</p><h2 id="notification-and-audit-retention" tabindex="-1">Notification and audit retention <a class="header-anchor" href="#notification-and-audit-retention" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Notification and audit retention&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Set these server-wide preferences in <strong>Settings → Storage</strong> or the JSON config:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Config key</th><th>Default</th><th>Allowed days</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>preferences.notification_retention_days</code></td><td>30</td><td>1–3650</td></tr><tr><td><code>preferences.audit_retention_days</code></td><td>90</td><td>1–3650</td></tr></tbody></table><p>All notifications expire, including unresolved requests&#39; alerts. Audit history retains the notification title and message independently until the audit event expires. The audit log is also capped at 200,000 rows, oldest removed first. Expiry filters apply immediately; the API performs bounded cleanup at startup and every minute. Freed SQLite pages are reusable but the file may not shrink. See <a href="./notifications">Notifications</a>.</p><h2 id="files-and-directories" tabindex="-1">Files and directories <a class="header-anchor" href="#files-and-directories" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Files and directories&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>Holds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>ORC_HOME/config.json</code></td><td>Global config. The CLI (<code>orc project use</code>), the dashboard and the desktop app write here.</td></tr><tr><td><code>./.orc/config.json</code></td><td>Optional config read from the directory the process starts in</td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_HOME/orc.db</code></td><td>SQLite database (<code>db.path</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_HOME/knowledge/</code></td><td>Knowledge base files and index (beside the database)</td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_HOME/secret.key</code></td><td>Key that encrypts stored secrets. Back it up with the database.</td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_HOME/logs/orc.log</code></td><td>Structured log</td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_HOME/daemon.log</code></td><td>Daemon stdout/stderr when run as a launchd or systemd service</td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_HOME/daemon.pid</code></td><td>PID of the running daemon</td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_HOME/daemon.json</code></td><td>Private runtime identity and current port of the running daemon; contains no credentials</td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_HOME/desktop-daemon.log</code></td><td>Daemon output when the desktop app started it</td></tr><tr><td><code>~/.orc/bin/</code></td><td>CLI installed by the desktop app</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>ORC_HOME</code> defaults to <code>~/.orc</code>, where <code>~</code> is <code>HOME</code> (<code>USERPROFILE</code> on Windows). Show the resolved paths and daemon state:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">orc</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> home</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="precedence" tabindex="-1">Precedence <a class="header-anchor" href="#precedence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Precedence&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Later wins:</p><ol><li><code>~/.orc/config.json</code></li><li><code>./.orc/config.json</code></li><li>Environment variables</li><li>Global CLI flags (<code>--port</code>, <code>--host</code>, <code>--db</code>, <code>--secret</code>, <code>--log-level</code>, <code>--runner-timeout</code>, <code>--runner-max-jobs</code>, <code>--snapshot-max-bytes</code>)</li></ol><p>An invalid config file stops ORC with <code>Invalid orc config</code> and the failing key.</p><h2 id="example-config" tabindex="-1">Example config <a class="header-anchor" href="#example-config" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Example config&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language-json vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">json</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">{</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">  &quot;api&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: { </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;port&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">7700</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;host&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;127.0.0.1&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;secret&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;change-me&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> },</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">  &quot;activeProject&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;my-app&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">,</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">  &quot;agent_loop&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: { </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;enabled&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">true</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;default_backend&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;claude&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;max_workers&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">2</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> },</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">  &quot;runner&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: { </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;default_timeout_secs&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">300</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;max_concurrent_jobs&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">5</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> },</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">  &quot;rules&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: { </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;enabled&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">true</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> }</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">}</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="config-keys" tabindex="-1">Config keys <a class="header-anchor" href="#config-keys" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Config keys&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="api" tabindex="-1"><code>api</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#api" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`api`&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Default</th><th>Env</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>api.port</code></td><td><code>7700</code></td><td><code>ORC_API_PORT</code></td><td>API, dashboard and MCP port</td></tr><tr><td><code>api.host</code></td><td><code>127.0.0.1</code></td><td><code>ORC_API_HOST</code></td><td>Bind address. <code>0.0.0.0</code> exposes ORC on the network.</td></tr><tr><td><code>api.secret</code></td><td>none</td><td><code>ORC_API_SECRET</code></td><td>Bearer secret. Unset means no auth.</td></tr><tr><td><code>api.session_days</code></td><td><code>30</code></td><td><code>ORC_API_SESSION_DAYS</code></td><td>Dashboard sign-in and default token lifetime (1-365)</td></tr><tr><td><code>api.cors_origins</code></td><td><code>[]</code></td><td><code>ORC_API_CORS_ORIGINS</code> (comma-separated)</td><td>Extra browser origins allowed to call the API</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="db-activeproject" tabindex="-1"><code>db</code>, <code>activeProject</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#db-activeproject" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`db`, `activeProject`&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Default</th><th>Env</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>db.path</code></td><td><code>ORC_HOME/orc.db</code></td><td><code>ORC_DB_PATH</code></td><td>SQLite file</td></tr><tr><td><code>activeProject</code></td><td>none</td><td><code>ORC_ACTIVE_PROJECT</code></td><td>Default project for CLI and MCP calls</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="agent-loop" tabindex="-1"><code>agent_loop</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#agent-loop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`agent_loop`&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The task loop picks up <code>todo</code> and <code>changes_requested</code> tasks and runs agents on them.</p><p><code>agent_loop.enabled</code> only turns the subsystem on at startup. Its cycles are recorded under the <code>orc-task-loop</code> system job, and disabling that job (Jobs page, <code>orc jobs</code>) stops every automatic cycle — scheduled ticks, task-change triggers and follow-ups — even while the setting is <code>true</code>. The disabled state survives restarts and re-enabling the job resumes the loop without an environment change. Disabling does not stop agents that are already running.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Default</th><th>Env</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>agent_loop.enabled</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td><code>ORC_AGENT_LOOP_ENABLED</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>agent_loop.poll_interval_minutes</code></td><td><code>5</code></td><td><code>ORC_AGENT_LOOP_POLL_INTERVAL</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>agent_loop.max_workers</code></td><td><code>1</code></td><td><code>ORC_AGENT_LOOP_MAX_WORKERS</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>agent_loop.default_backend</code></td><td><code>claude</code></td><td><code>ORC_AGENT_LOOP_DEFAULT_BACKEND</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>agent_loop.session_idle_timeout_minutes</code></td><td><code>20</code></td><td><code>ORC_AGENT_LOOP_IDLE_TIMEOUT</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>agent_loop.session_max_lifetime_minutes</code></td><td><code>120</code></td><td><code>ORC_AGENT_LOOP_MAX_LIFETIME</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>agent_loop.worker_auto_approve</code></td><td><code>true</code></td><td><code>ORC_AGENT_LOOP_AUTO_APPROVE</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>agent_loop.default_flow</code></td><td><code>orc-default</code></td><td><code>ORC_AGENT_LOOP_DEFAULT_FLOW</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>agent_loop.review_flow</code></td><td><code>orc-review-only</code></td><td><code>ORC_AGENT_LOOP_REVIEW_FLOW</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>agent_loop.max_node_retries</code></td><td><code>2</code></td><td><code>ORC_AGENT_LOOP_MAX_NODE_RETRIES</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>agent_loop.max_flow_runs_per_task</code></td><td><code>6</code></td><td><code>ORC_AGENT_LOOP_MAX_FLOW_RUNS</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Run <code>orc doctor</code> to see which backends are usable. See <a href="./task-flows">Task flows</a>.</p><h3 id="runner-jobs" tabindex="-1"><code>runner</code> (jobs) <a class="header-anchor" href="#runner-jobs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`runner` (jobs)&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Default</th><th>Env</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>runner.default_timeout_secs</code></td><td><code>300</code></td><td><code>ORC_RUNNER_TIMEOUT</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>runner.max_concurrent_jobs</code></td><td><code>5</code></td><td><code>ORC_RUNNER_MAX_JOBS</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>runner.log_retention_days</code></td><td><code>30</code></td><td><code>ORC_RUNNER_LOG_DAYS</code></td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="context-memory" tabindex="-1"><code>context</code>, <code>memory</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#context-memory" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`context`, `memory`&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Default</th><th>Env</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>context.snapshot_max_bytes</code></td><td><code>2048</code></td><td><code>ORC_SNAPSHOT_MAX_BYTES</code></td><td>Session snapshot budget</td></tr><tr><td><code>context.layer1_task_limit</code></td><td><code>10</code></td><td><code>ORC_LAYER1_TASKS</code></td><td>Tasks in the <code>context</code> result</td></tr><tr><td><code>context.layer1_memory_limit</code></td><td><code>30</code></td><td><code>ORC_LAYER1_MEMORIES</code></td><td>Memories in the <code>context</code> result</td></tr><tr><td><code>memory.max_content_chars</code></td><td><code>800</code></td><td>none</td><td>Longest memory content</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="knowledge" tabindex="-1"><code>knowledge</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#knowledge" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`knowledge`&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Default</th><th>Env</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>knowledge.root</code></td><td>beside <code>db.path</code></td><td><code>ORC_KNOWLEDGE_ROOT</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>knowledge.parser</code></td><td><code>auto</code></td><td><code>ORC_KNOWLEDGE_PARSER</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>knowledge.embedding.provider</code></td><td><code>none</code> (<code>none</code>, <code>ollama</code>, <code>openai</code>)</td><td><code>ORC_KNOWLEDGE_EMBEDDING_PROVIDER</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>knowledge.embedding.base_url</code></td><td>provider default</td><td><code>ORC_KNOWLEDGE_EMBEDDING_URL</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>knowledge.embedding.model</code></td><td>provider default</td><td><code>ORC_KNOWLEDGE_EMBEDDING_MODEL</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>knowledge.embedding.api_key</code></td><td>none</td><td><code>ORC_KNOWLEDGE_EMBEDDING_API_KEY</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>knowledge.max_upload_bytes</code></td><td><code>104857600</code> (100 MB)</td><td>none</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Most knowledge settings are also editable under <strong>Settings → Knowledge</strong>.</p><h3 id="terminals-ssh" tabindex="-1"><code>terminals</code>, <code>ssh</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#terminals-ssh" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`terminals`, `ssh`&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Default</th><th>Env</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>terminals.enabled</code></td><td><code>true</code></td><td><code>ORC_TERMINALS_ENABLED</code></td><td>Browser terminals</td></tr><tr><td><code>terminals.max</code></td><td><code>8</code></td><td>none</td><td>Concurrent terminals (1-64)</td></tr><tr><td><code>terminals.shell</code></td><td>unset</td><td><code>ORC_TERMINALS_SHELL</code></td><td>Shell executable, or a JSON array of executable and literal arguments</td></tr><tr><td><code>ssh.host</code></td><td>none</td><td><code>ORC_SSH_HOST</code></td><td><code>~/.ssh/config</code> host alias to run agents, terminals and git on</td></tr><tr><td><code>ssh.remote_port</code></td><td>none</td><td><code>ORC_SSH_REMOTE_PORT</code></td><td>Port the reverse tunnel opens on the SSH host so agents there reach ORC</td></tr></tbody></table><p>In JSON configuration, <code>&quot;terminals&quot;: { &quot;shell&quot;: [&quot;pwsh&quot;, &quot;-NoLogo&quot;, &quot;-NoProfile&quot;] }</code> launches PowerShell without personal profiles. The first array entry must be a nonempty executable; up to 64 entries are supported. Arguments are passed literally, without shell parsing. Existing executable strings remain supported, including <code>ORC_TERMINALS_SHELL</code>; that environment variable accepts an executable, not a command line or JSON array. An unset shell keeps the platform default, including its usual profile loading.</p><p>Pick the execution host from <strong>Settings → Execution host</strong>. Switching applies to new actions. When <code>ORC_SSH_HOST</code> is set, the selection is fixed.</p><h3 id="rules-mcp-github" tabindex="-1"><code>rules</code>, <code>mcp</code>, <code>github</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#rules-mcp-github" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`rules`, `mcp`, `github`&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Default</th><th>Env</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>rules.enabled</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td><code>ORC_RULES_ENABLED</code></td><td>Enforce <a href="./agent-rules">agent rules</a></td></tr><tr><td><code>mcp.default_mode</code></td><td><code>meta</code></td><td>none</td><td>MCP tool surface: <code>meta</code> or <code>full</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>github.token</code></td><td>none</td><td><code>ORC_GITHUB_TOKEN</code></td><td>Token for ORC&#39;s GitHub calls (pull requests, reviews)</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="gateway-telegram-slack" tabindex="-1"><code>gateway</code> (Telegram, Slack) <a class="header-anchor" href="#gateway-telegram-slack" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`gateway` (Telegram, Slack)&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Env</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>ORC_TELEGRAM_TOKEN</code></td><td>Enables the Telegram gateway with this bot token</td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN</code>, <code>ORC_SLACK_APP_TOKEN</code></td><td>Enables the Slack gateway (Socket Mode)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Channels are configured under <strong>Settings → Channels</strong>.</p><h3 id="speech-tts" tabindex="-1"><code>speech</code>, <code>tts</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#speech-tts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`speech`, `tts`&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Env</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>ORC_SPEECH_PROVIDER</code></td><td><code>openai</code>, <code>groq</code> or <code>qwen</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_SPEECH_LANGUAGE</code></td><td>Transcription language</td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_TTS_PROVIDER</code></td><td><code>openai</code> or <code>qwen</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_TTS_VOICE</code></td><td>Voice (default <code>alloy</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_TTS_MODE</code></td><td><code>voice_only</code> or <code>always</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_OPENAI_API_KEY</code>, <code>ORC_GROQ_API_KEY</code>, <code>ORC_QWEN_API_KEY</code></td><td>Speech provider keys</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Setting any <code>ORC_SPEECH_*</code> or provider key enables speech; setting any <code>ORC_TTS_*</code> enables text-to-speech.</p><h2 id="other-environment-variables" tabindex="-1">Other environment variables <a class="header-anchor" href="#other-environment-variables" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Other environment variables&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Default</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>ORC_HOME</code></td><td><code>~/.orc</code></td><td>Data root: location of the database, <code>config.json</code>, <code>secret.key</code>, logs and the daemon files (<code>daemon.pid</code>, <code>daemon.json</code>, <code>daemon.log</code>, <code>desktop-daemon.log</code>).</td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_API_BASE</code></td><td>from <code>api.host</code>/<code>api.port</code></td><td>ORC origin for the CLI, stdio MCP and hooks</td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_SESSION_ID</code></td><td>none</td><td>Session attribution for MCP and CLI writes</td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_LOG_LEVEL</code></td><td><code>info</code></td><td><code>debug</code>, <code>info</code>, <code>warn</code>, <code>error</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_LOG_DIR</code></td><td><code>ORC_HOME</code></td><td>Parent of the <code>logs/</code> directory</td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_LOG_FILE</code></td><td>on</td><td><code>0</code> disables the log file</td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_WEB_DIST</code></td><td>bundled</td><td>Serve a different dashboard build</td></tr><tr><td><code>ORC_SQLITE_VEC_PATH</code></td><td>bundled</td><td>sqlite-vec library for vector search</td></tr><tr><td><code>AGENTAPI_URL</code></td><td><code>http://localhost:3284</code></td><td>agentapi server for the <code>agentapi</code> backend</td></tr><tr><td><code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code></td><td>none</td><td><code>claude</code> backend</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="ports" tabindex="-1">Ports <a class="header-anchor" href="#ports" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Ports&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Port</th><th>Serves</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>7700</code></td><td>REST API (<code>/api/*</code>), dashboard (<code>/</code>), MCP (<code>/mcp</code>), OpenAPI (<code>/openapi.json</code>), Swagger UI (<code>/docs</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Change it with <code>api.port</code>, <code>ORC_API_PORT</code> or <code>orc --port &lt;n&gt; daemon start</code>. The desktop app reads the same setting.</p><p>Desktop automatically chooses an available loopback port if another application occupies this port and no daemon is already running. It saves the selected port and loopback host in global config after a successful start, so default CLI and stdio MCP connections follow it. Explicit overrides still win. See <a href="./desktop-and-web#desktop-app-only">Desktop app</a> for reuse and recovery behavior.</p><p>The daemon writes <code>daemon.json</code> next to <code>daemon.pid</code> once its API is listening. The record contains its PID, bound port, instance ID, version, database/config paths, start time and whether authentication is enabled, never the secret. It is written atomically with user-only file permissions and removed on clean shutdown. Desktop ignores stale records whose PID is no longer the active daemon.</p><p>Health check (no auth needed):</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">curl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -s</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> http://127.0.0.1:7700/api/health</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="logs" tabindex="-1">Logs <a class="header-anchor" href="#logs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Logs&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>~/.orc/logs/orc.log</code> holds JSON lines. It rotates at 10 MB and keeps three rotated files (<code>orc.log.1</code> to <code>orc.log.3</code>). The terminal gets the same entries in readable form on stderr.</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">grep</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;&quot;level&quot;:&quot;error&quot;&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ~/.orc/logs/orc.log</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> tail</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -20</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">tail</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -f</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ~/.orc/logs/orc.log</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> jq</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> .</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">orc</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --log-level</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> debug</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> daemon</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> start</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="run-as-a-background-service" tabindex="-1">Run as a background service <a class="header-anchor" href="#run-as-a-background-service" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Run as a background service&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>orc daemon start</code> runs in the foreground. To start it at login and keep it running:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">orc</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> daemon</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> install</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">     # register and start now</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">orc</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> daemon</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> uninstall</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">   # remove the registration</span></span></code></pre></div><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Restarts on crash</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>macOS</td><td>launchd agent <code>~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.orc.daemon.plist</code></td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Linux</td><td>systemd user service <code>~/.config/systemd/user/orc-daemon.service</code></td><td>Yes (on failure)</td></tr><tr><td>Windows</td><td>Registry Run key <code>HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run\\OrcDaemon</code></td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table><p>No admin rights needed. Service output goes to <code>~/.orc/daemon.log</code> on macOS and Linux.</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">orc</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> daemon</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> stop</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">                       # stop the running daemon</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">orc</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> daemon</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> status</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">                     # list scheduled jobs and their last runs</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">orc</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> status</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">                            # API health and counts</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">launchctl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> list</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> grep</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> orc</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">             # macOS</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">systemctl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --user</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> status</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> orc-daemon</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">    # Linux</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>orc daemon install</code> refuses an <code>orc</code> that lives under nvm, fnm, volta, asdf or nodenv paths. Install a <a href="./install#pre-built-binary">pre-built binary</a>, or pass <code>--force</code>.</p><p><code>orc daemon start</code> merges your login shell&#39;s <code>PATH</code> on macOS and Linux, so agent CLIs such as <code>claude</code>, <code>codex</code> and <code>cursor-agent</code> are found when the daemon runs as a service.</p><p>If a daemon already answers on the port, the desktop app uses it instead of starting its own.</p><p><code>orc api</code> starts only the API server, without the scheduler, watchers, gateway or task loop.</p><h2 id="authentication" tabindex="-1">Authentication <a class="header-anchor" href="#authentication" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Authentication&quot;">​</a></h2><p>With no secret, ORC accepts every request. Keep <code>api.host</code> on <code>127.0.0.1</code> in that case.</p><p>Set a secret:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">orc</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --secret</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;$(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">openssl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> rand </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">-hex</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 32</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">)&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> daemon</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> start</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">   # one run</span></span></code></pre></div><p>or put it in <code>~/.orc/config.json</code> as <code>api.secret</code>, or set <code>ORC_API_SECRET</code>. The desktop app generates and saves one for you.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Client</th><th>How it authenticates</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CLI, stdio MCP on the same machine</td><td>Reads the secret from config or <code>ORC_API_SECRET</code></td></tr><tr><td>HTTP clients, HTTP MCP</td><td><code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;secret or access token&gt;</code></td></tr><tr><td>Dashboard</td><td>Asks for the secret once and stores a signed HttpOnly cookie for <code>api.session_days</code></td></tr><tr><td>Desktop app</td><td>Sends the secret it configured</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Unauthenticated paths: <code>/health</code>, <code>/api/health</code>, <code>/api/auth/session</code>, <code>/api/auth/token</code>. Handoff upload, session-sync, skill download and scratchpad content URLs skip the secret but check their own signed, expiring link.</p><h3 id="access-tokens" tabindex="-1">Access tokens <a class="header-anchor" href="#access-tokens" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Access tokens&quot;">​</a></h3><p>An access token is a signed bearer credential that expires. Use one instead of the raw secret for agents and scripts.</p><p>Create one under <strong>Settings → Security</strong>, or:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">curl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -s</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -X</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> POST</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> http://127.0.0.1:7700/api/auth/token</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> \\</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">  -H</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;Content-Type: application/json&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> \\</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">  -d</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;{&quot;secret&quot;:&quot;&#39;&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">$ORC_API_SECRET</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;&#39;&quot;}&#39;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Returns <code>{&quot;token&quot;: &quot;...&quot;, &quot;expires_at&quot;: &lt;unix seconds&gt;}</code>. Pass <code>expires_at</code> (unix seconds) to choose the expiry, up to 365 days. Rotating the secret revokes every token.</p><p>For exposing ORC beyond localhost, see <a href="./self-hosted">Self-hosting</a>.</p><h3 id="direct-acp-commands" tabindex="-1">Direct ACP commands <a class="header-anchor" href="#direct-acp-commands" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Direct ACP commands&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>agent_loop.acp_commands</code> optionally maps agent IDs to <code>{ &quot;command&quot;: &quot;executable&quot;, &quot;args&quot;: [&quot;literal&quot;, &quot;arguments&quot;] }</code>. Add the ID to <code>preferences.agents</code> to select it in chat. The <code>opencode</code> agent already ships a built-in direct ACP command (<code>opencode acp</code>); an <code>acp_commands.opencode</code> entry overrides it, for example to pin a specific installation. Commands run on the chosen execution host; authenticate using that agent&#39;s own login or existing secure environment configuration. Arguments are not shell expressions and must not contain secrets. See <a href="./connect-agents#capabilities-and-direct-acp">Connect agents</a> for setup, capability negotiation and permission limitations. Existing chats retain their selected backend even if this configuration changes.</p>',130)])])}const m=t(i,[["render",r]]);export{k as __pageData,m as default};
